From ef696cba1a98b4478d2859d866cdb3e31cf6a897 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Facilis Bot <dev@facilis.local>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 18:07:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(opfs): drain project paths ahead of package-manager
 caches

The write-behind mirror persists the package-manager caches, and a cold pnpm
install enqueues thousands of store files. Two consequences, both observed in a
real browser session: a project write made during the install sat behind the
whole cache queue (a reload discards the in-memory queue, so the edit was lost),
and the manifest was rewritten only once the queue drained empty, so the index
on disk stayed minutes behind the files OPFS already held.

Route cache paths to a second, low-priority queue; drain it only when nothing
project-level is queued, and rewrite the manifest while the queue is busy (at
most once per second, the existing debounce). flush() on page-hide now promises
the project paths plus the index and leaves the cache backlog queued, so a
hiding tab finishes quickly instead of waiting for an install to settle.

Verified in a browser WebContainer (Facilis IDE smoke, 24/24 write checks,
including "uncommitted file survives the reboot", previously a known upstream
failure): before the change OPFS kept 514 entries out of 8,000+ pending and the
probe file was never indexed; after it the probe survives the reload. Offline
regression: node scripts/spike-opfs-delete-recreate.mjs.
